WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
The Lord of the Rings trilogy is littered&nbsp;
with epic sacrifices, gruesome deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.240
and both happy and sad endings. From&nbsp;
mighty warriors to slimy bad guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.320
here are the last words of these fallen&nbsp;
Lord of the Rings heroes and villains.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.560
Before we can discuss final&nbsp;
words, it's important to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.440
which final words we're talking about.&nbsp;
After all, in addition to author J.R.R.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.480
Tolkien's original books, there's the animated&nbsp;
Lord of the Rings films created in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.440
'70s and early '80s, and of course,&nbsp;
Peter Jackson's live-action trilogy.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.800
In this video, we'll go with a combination&nbsp;
of the live-action adaptations and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.840
original books. However, some of the fallen&nbsp;
characters still don't have concrete "last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.200
words" in either version. When this happens,&nbsp;
we'll give a brief rundown of their final moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.040
After all, actions speak louder than words.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.840
While not a primary character in The Lord&nbsp;
of the Rings, Isildur shows up early in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.560
Fellowship of the Ring film and is referenced&nbsp;
throughout the narrative. The ancient warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.400
who cut the One Ring from Sauron's hand, Isildur&nbsp;
dies when he's ambushed by orcs, while trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.160
escape across the Great River. In the film,&nbsp;
his last word comes long before his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.560
when he rejects Elrond's plea for him to&nbsp;
throw the Ring into the fires of Mount Doom.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:19.920
"Destroy it!"

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.800
"No."

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.320
However, in Tolkien's book Unfinished Tales,&nbsp;
Isildur's final line is a bit juicier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.480
Knowing he's about to be killed,&nbsp;
Isildur's son, Elendur tells his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.440
father to bring the One Ring to the elves&nbsp;
before it's recaptured. His father replies,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.040
"I knew that I must do so; but I feared the pain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.480
Nor could I go without your leave. Forgive me,&nbsp;
and my pride that has brought you to this doom."

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.880
Isildur's father, Elendil, is the first king of&nbsp;
the realms of Gondor and Arnor at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.120
Second Age. During the same time, Gil-galad is an&nbsp;
elven leader who rules over the bulk of the elves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.960
that remain in Middle-earth. Eventually, Sauron&nbsp;
picks a fight with them, and the two leaders form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.160
the Last Alliance, a massive coalition that&nbsp;
gathers together to attack Sauron in Mordor.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:16.000
The Last Alliance is ultimately victorious but&nbsp;
at the expense of both of these high kings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.560
They are killed in combat with Sauron&nbsp;
just before Isildur cuts the One Ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.600
from the villain's hand using&nbsp;
his father's broken sword.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.600
While neither character is officially given final&nbsp;
words, they appear in both the books and films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.400
Their brave actions set the stage for Sauron's&nbsp;
first defeat at the end of the Second Age.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.120
Everyone is fascinated by Sméagol, the hobbit-like&nbsp;
creature eventually known as Gollum. He steals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.520
the One Ring from a friend on his birthday,&nbsp;
and in the process, murders his companion.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.200
That friend is Déagol, another hobbit-like&nbsp;
creature. Five centuries before the Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.720
of the Rings story begins, Déagol finds the&nbsp;
One Ring while out fishing with his friend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.920
When Sméagol sees the Ring, he demands that&nbsp;
Déagol give it to him since it's his birthday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.120
In the opening scene of Return of the King, the&nbsp;
poor fellow's response winds up being his last.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:14.480
"Give us that here, my love."

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:14.980
"Why?"

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.280
In the book, Déagol says a&nbsp;
little more, telling Sméagol,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.920
"I don't care. I have given you a present already,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.440
more than I could afford. I found&nbsp;
this, and I'm going to keep it."

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.240
Sadly, poor Déagol doesn't realize&nbsp;
the tragic irony in his statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.600
and winds up paying a much greater price.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.640
Although Gandalf doesn't technically die, he&nbsp;
certainly gives everyone the impression that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:45.840
does, so his seemingly final words are still worth&nbsp;
noting. While staring down the Balrog he shouts,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.520
"You shall not pass!"

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.200
Unfortunately, although Gandalf&nbsp;
successfully defeats the Balrog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.640
the fiery monster winds up&nbsp;
taking the wizard down with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.880
As Gandalf realizes he's about to&nbsp;
fall, he cries out to his friends,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:04.640
"Fly, you fools!"

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.800
Identical in both book and movie, the words&nbsp;
communicate a sense of care and frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.760
which pushes the rest of the Fellowship members&nbsp;
into action. While he does return later in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.880
story, Gandalf's sacrifice is still a powerful&nbsp;
moment that's earned a spot on this list.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.440
Also known as Durin's Bane, the fiery&nbsp;
Balrog demon seems indestructibly powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.320
in The Fellowship of the Ring, but the&nbsp;
book reveals he's actually a refugee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.480
fleeing from the catastrophic War of Wrath. In&nbsp;
that war, Sauron's original master, Morgoth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:44.240
is defeated, and his surviving servants scatter&nbsp;
across the continent, looking for places to hide.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:50.000
The Balrog relocates deep beneath the dwarf&nbsp;
kingdom of Khazad-dûm for nearly 2,000 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.200
Eventually, he's disturbed by the dwarves,&nbsp;
at which point he drives them away, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.440
area becomes known as Moria or the "Black Pit."&nbsp;
When the Balrog attacks the Fellowship, Gandalf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.960
confronts the demon on the Bridge of Khazad-dûm,&nbsp;
and sends them both tumbling into the abyss below.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.880
As they fall, they begin an intense duel that&nbsp;
progresses from deep underground all the way up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.160
to the top of the mountains above. There, with&nbsp;
clouds, fire, and lightning surrounding them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.160
the Balrog is finally killed by Gandalf. The&nbsp;
Balrog doesn't get any last words, but its death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.920
clears the way for the dwarves to eventually&nbsp;
return to their ancient home of Khazad-dûm.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.720
While most of the main characters in Peter&nbsp;
Jackson's films are from Tolkien's original text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.640
the uruk-hai captain Lurtz was&nbsp;
entirely Jackson's creation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.960
Lurtz leads Saruman's forces in pursuit of the&nbsp;
Fellowship, and provides an effective foe for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.400
the main characters to defeat in the otherwise&nbsp;
villain-less finale of The Fellowship of the Ring.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.800
After shooting Boromir full of arrows, the orc&nbsp;
captain takes on Aragorn in a one-on-one duel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.840
After a harrowing showdown, the Ranger wins out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.600
and Lurtz meets his demise with his&nbsp;
arm and head removed from his body.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.000
Lurtz doesn't have many lines in the&nbsp;
film, and doesn't say anything at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.720
as he quietly duels multiple members of the&nbsp;
Fellowship during the film's ending battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.840
However, shortly before his last breath, the&nbsp;
uruk-hai can be heard shouting to his men,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.560
"Find the halflings. Find the halflings!"

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.040
Created with only one purpose, Lurtz's&nbsp;
final words are focused on his mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.240
just like his whole life.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.800
Heir to the steward of Gondor, Boromir joins the&nbsp;
Fellowship of the Ring only to succumb to the lure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.560
of the Ring. But after attempting to steal it&nbsp;
from Frodo, Boromir repents and sacrifices his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.000
life defending Merry and Pippin. Moments before&nbsp;
he takes his final breath, Boromir confesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.880
to Aragorn that he tried to take the Ring,&nbsp;
and that Merry and Pippin have been kidnapped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.920
He also urges Aragorn to go to help his&nbsp;
people, before uttering his final words.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:03.200
"I would have followed you,&nbsp;
my brother.... My captain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:07.920
my king."

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.040
The revelation that Boromir finally&nbsp;
accepted Aragorn as his king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.760
is the perfect way to resolve his previous&nbsp;
scorn for the Ranger at the Council of Elrond.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.440
In the books, Boromir's final line reads,

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.080
"Farewell, Aragorn! Go to Minas Tirith&nbsp;
and save my people! I have failed."

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.640
Aragorn responds that Boromir&nbsp;
has not failed, but conquered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.960
prompting a smile from Boromir&nbsp;
before he breathes his last.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.880
In Tolkien's books, the elf Haldir helps the&nbsp;
Fellowship out as they travel through Lothlorien.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:44.960
But after the travelers depart down the&nbsp;
Great River, we don't ever meet him again.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.640
In the movies, though, Peter Jackson brought back&nbsp;
the Lothlorien guide for the climactic battle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.160
The Two Towers. As the men of Rohan prepare&nbsp;
to fend off Saruman's hordes at Helm's Deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.800
Haldir unexpectedly shows up with&nbsp;
a bunch of high-quality archers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.000
Unfortunately, in the ensuing battle,&nbsp;
Haldir meets an untimely death.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.040
Just before Haldir's death, he shouts something&nbsp;
in his native tongue to his retreating men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.160
However, since we aren't sure of the translation,&nbsp;
we'll instead highlight the last thing he says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:22.080
after arriving with his troops. Facing King&nbsp;
Théoden, he says he brings word from Elrond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.080
saying that they wish to honor the&nbsp;
old alliance between men and elves.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.920
"We are proud to fight alongside men once more."

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.600
Originally sent to resist Sauron, Saruman the&nbsp;
White Wizard eventually becomes corrupted and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.720
vies for power himself. This leads to&nbsp;
his overthrow in The Two Towers and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.720
eventual death at the hands of Gríma&nbsp;
Wormtongue in The Return of the King.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.480
In the extended film version, Saruman's death&nbsp;
comes as he stands on top of the Tower of Orthanc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.760
in the midst of the drowned ring of Isengard.&nbsp;
As he argues with Gandalf and his allies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.640
Wormtongue attacks him from behind, sending&nbsp;
him careening off the edge of the tower.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.400
Just before he's killed, Saruman&nbsp;
can be heard saying his last words,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.640
"You withdraw your guard, and I will&nbsp;
tell you where your doom will be decided.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.600
I will not be held prisoner here."

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:19.440
In the books, Saruman survives until the end of&nbsp;
The Return of the King. By then, he's going by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.320
the name "Sharkey", whom Frodo and his friends&nbsp;
discover has orchestrated the destruction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:30.240
Shire. Defeated at last by the hobbits, Saruman&nbsp;
orders a broken Wormtongue to follow him, saying,

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.560
"You do what Sharkey says, always, don't&nbsp;
you, Worm? Well, now he says, follow!"

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:39.680
At that, the Wormtongue of the books finally&nbsp;
turns on his master and slits his throat.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.720
We first meet Gríma Wormtongue&nbsp;
secretly betraying his king, Théoden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.240
After his duplicity is discovered, he openly&nbsp;
allies himself with the powerful Saruman, fleeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:56.320
to the White Wizard's domain at Isengard. There,&nbsp;
he serves his new master faithfully yet miserably.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:02.400
In the films, he meets his ending early in the&nbsp;
extended version of The Return of the King. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.800
stabbing Saruman in a fit of rage, Wormtongue&nbsp;
is felled by an arrow fired by Legolas. His last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:14.000
word, uttered moments before, is in defiance&nbsp;
of Saruman's dismal prediction of his future.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.840
"Free? He will never be free."

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:18.340
"No."

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.840
In the book The Return of the King, a&nbsp;
fed-up Gríma backstabs Saruman again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.760
this time in the Shire after his&nbsp;
master is defeated by the hobbits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.440
Just before he slits Saruman's&nbsp;
throat and is shot by arrows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.880
Wormtongue is accused by his master of murdering&nbsp;
Frodo's cousin, to which he violently hisses,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.120
"You told me to; you made me do it."

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.200
Théoden is already quite old when Gandalf frees&nbsp;
him from the spells of his counselor, Gríma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:51.520
Wormtongue. After regaining control of himself,&nbsp;
the king of Rohan leads his armies to Helm's Deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.480
where they win an epic victory by the skin of&nbsp;
their teeth. They then head to Minas Tirith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:02.320
where the aged ruler dies in a glorious charge&nbsp;
that kicks off the Battle of the Pelennor Fields.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:06.800
It's none other than the Witch-king himself&nbsp;
who kills Théoden in battle. In the movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:10.800
his airborne beast lands on the king,&nbsp;
whipping him around and sending both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:16.400
Théoden and his horse flying to their deaths.&nbsp;
In the books, it's clarified that the Witch-king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:20.960
actually kills Théoden's horse, who then&nbsp;
falls on the king, crushing him to death.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.560
After Éowyn and Merry defeat the Witch-king&nbsp;
together, we get two different sets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.320
Théoden's final words. In the film The&nbsp;
Return of the King, Théoden says to Éowyn,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:41.840
"I go to my fathers, in whose mighty&nbsp;
company I shall not now feel ashamed."
 &nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:48.480
He then whispers her name one last time before&nbsp;
he dies. In the book, Théoden doesn't realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.840
that Éowyn is nearby. His final words are&nbsp;
spoken to her brother, Éomer, when he says,

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.600
"Hail, king of the Mark! Ride now&nbsp;
to victory! Bid Éowyn farewell!"

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.840
Both the book and the film give Théoden satisfying&nbsp;
last words, worthy of the noble king he was.

00:12:06.876 --> 00:12:11.680
The millenia-old Witch-king is a recurring&nbsp;
villain throughout the Lord of the Rings trilogy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.360
He leads the nine Ringwraiths on&nbsp;
their deadly hunt for the Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.080
stabs Frodo at Weathertop, and leads the&nbsp;
massive army that attacks Minas Tirith.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.640
After wreaking havoc on Middle Earth&nbsp;
for thousands of years, the Witch-king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.000
finally meets his end during the Battle of the&nbsp;
Pelennor Fields. Overconfident from millennia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.040
of successes and a prophecy that no living&nbsp;
man can kill him, he recklessly gets involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:40.720
in the fighting. After mortally wounding King&nbsp;
Théoden, he is confronted by Éowyn and Merry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:46.560
who work together to finish him off. In the&nbsp;
movie, his ironic last words to Éowyn are,

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:53.840
"No man can kill me. Die now."

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:58.320
In the books, his final&nbsp;
words are a little different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:02.960
but still set up Éowyn's iconic response,&nbsp;
before she strikes the killing blow.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.840
"I am no man."

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.640
Although he's barely mentioned in the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.400
Gothmog is second-in-command&nbsp;
during the siege of Minas Tirith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:21.920
However, in the film version of The Return of the&nbsp;
King, Gothmog gets a much larger role as a leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.640
high on his own authority who is convinced&nbsp;
that the age of the orc is about to begin.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:31.360
His involvement in the films makes him a big&nbsp;
enough character to deserve a spot on this list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.200
In The Return of the King, we see&nbsp;
him ordering his soldiers around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.200
dodging boulders, and commanding his men&nbsp;
to brace for the charge of the Rohirrim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.240
Later in the battle, Gothmog can be&nbsp;
seen crawling towards the wounded Éowyn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:49.360
With his weapon lifted to kill, Aragorn and Gimli&nbsp;
arrive in the nick of time and finish him off.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:53.680
In order to catch his last words,&nbsp;
you need to back up to just before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:56.800
the Riders of Rohan come crashing into his army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.840
Prepping his troops for the incoming tidal&nbsp;
wave, his last words are the doomed command,

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.840
"Fire at will!"

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:10.480
As the steward of Gondor, Denethor is in charge&nbsp;
of leading Gondor in the absence of the true king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:15.280
Eventually, he breaks down under the&nbsp;
pressure of the ongoing attack from Mordor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:18.400
and tries to burn himself&nbsp;
and his son, Faramir alive.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:23.520
In the movies, as Gandalf and Pippin&nbsp;
arrive to stop him from murdering his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.400
Denethor's desperate near-final words are,

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.960
"You will not take my son from me!"

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.640
After Gandalf knocks him back onto the&nbsp;
blazing pyre, Denethor gazes on his son and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:39.920
quietly utters his name as his last word, before&nbsp;
running from the room and falling to his death.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.000
"Faramir."

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:47.600
In the books, Denethor's last lines are&nbsp;
directed at Gandalf before he lights the pyre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.440
and then to his servants, saying,

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:52.400
"But in this at least thou shalt not defy my will:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:56.960
to rule my own end. [...] Come hither!&nbsp;
Come, if you are not all recreant!"

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:02.560
After this, he sets the pyre on fire&nbsp;
and purposefully lies down to die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.960
As he lies there, he gives one great&nbsp;
cry and then perishes amidst the flames.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.600
Although he doesn't appear in the theatrical&nbsp;
version of The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:16.560
the Mouth of Sauron makes a memorable&nbsp;
appearance in the extended edition of the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:21.040
A powerful official in the Dark Lord's&nbsp;
bureaucracy, he comes out to haggle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:26.160
the captains of the West when they arrive at the&nbsp;
gates of Mordor. In the books, while the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:31.360
almost certainly dies when Sauron is defeated,&nbsp;
he doesn't get any definitive last words.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.760
In the film, though, he's beheaded by&nbsp;
Aragorn after foolishly taunting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:41.440
long-lost king of Gondor. His last words&nbsp;
before he's unceremoniously executed are,

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:48.320
"And who is this? Isildur's heir? It takes more&nbsp;
to make a king than a broken elvish blade."

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:54.160
Gollum's last words come during&nbsp;
his moment of greatest triumph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:59.600
right before he meets his demise. The&nbsp;
single-minded creature inadvertently saves the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:05.600
by attacking Frodo Baggins in Mount Doom when he&nbsp;
chooses to claim the Ring rather than destroy it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:10.480
Biting off Frodo's ring finger, Gollum&nbsp;
finally reclaims his most prized possession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:14.160
but then slips off the edge of the&nbsp;
cliff and falls to a fiery death.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:17.840
In the film The Return of the&nbsp;
King, his final words are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.840
victorious chant before Frodo tackles&nbsp;
him and they both tumble off the cliff.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:30.720
"Yes! Precious, precious, precious, precious!"

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:36.640
In the book, Gollum also gleefully repeats the&nbsp;
word "precious" over and over. But in Tolkien's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:42.560
version, he then steps backward just a tad too&nbsp;
far and slips to his death, without any help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:48.120
from Frodo. His final word comes out of the&nbsp;
depths as he wails "precious" one last time.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:55.120
While Sauron doesn't technically "die," nor have&nbsp;
any recorded last words, his ending is still worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:00.560
noting as the main antagonist of the trilogy.&nbsp;
Whether you're talking about his fiery eye falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:06.160
from a crumbling tower in the films, or the more&nbsp;
spiritual implication of his defeat in the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:09.520
his epic fall marks the&nbsp;
climax of the entire trilogy.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.440
In the book The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:15.920
Gandalf explains what will happen to Sauron&nbsp;
after the Ring is destroyed, saying that,

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.680
"If [the Ring] is destroyed, then [Sauron]&nbsp;
will fall; and his fall will be so low that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:26.720
none can foresee his arising ever again. [...]&nbsp;
He will lose the best part of the strength that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:32.160
was native to him in his beginning, and all that&nbsp;
was made or begun with that power will crumble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:37.520
and he will be maimed forever, becoming a&nbsp;
mere spirit of malice that gnaws itself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.240
the shadows, but cannot again grow or take shape."

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:44.800
So while Sauron may not be&nbsp;
dead or have formal last words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.720
there's no doubt that his fall marks&nbsp;
a turning point for Middle Earth.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.000
Grishnákh is an orc captain that shows&nbsp;
up for a brief stint in The Two Towers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.440
when Merry and Pippin are being&nbsp;
dragged across Rohan. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:03.280
he's the chief of the soldiers who serve the&nbsp;
Dark Lord. He resists the Uruk-hai captain Uglúk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:08.800
who wants to speed the prisoners to Saruman&nbsp;
in Isengard rather than Sauron in Mordor.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:15.520
In Tolkien's original iteration, Grishnákh tries&nbsp;
to spirit away the Hobbits from Uglúk's soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:21.120
so he can have the One Ring for himself. In&nbsp;
this process, we hear his last words, which are,

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:26.000
"Untie your legs? I'll untie every string&nbsp;
in your bodies. Do you think I can't search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:30.640
you to the bones? Search you! I'll&nbsp;
cut you both to quivering shreds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:34.560
I don't need the help of your legs to get&nbsp;
you away — and have you all to myself!"

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:39.040
After this, he tries to escape with&nbsp;
both Hobbits, but he's ridden down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:43.600
skewered by a Rider of Rohan, bringing&nbsp;
his chapter-long role to a sudden end.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:49.360
In Peter Jackson's adaptation, the villain's&nbsp;
fate is even worse. He survives the Rohirrim raid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:54.400
and chases the Hobbits into Fangorn Forest.&nbsp;
Limping and already bloodied from wounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.560
he yanks Merry out of a tree and says,

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.520
"Let's put a maggot hole in your belly."

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.120
Unfortunately for Grishnákh, he doesn't&nbsp;
realize Treebeard is waking up behind him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:09.840
and the Ent promptly stomps him out of existence.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:15.440
Háma is the doorward of Edoras, charged&nbsp;
with guarding Théoden's house. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:20.720
it turns out he isn't great at his job, since he&nbsp;
lets Gandalf into the hall with his staff in hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:24.640
inadvertently enabling the wizard to free&nbsp;
Théoden from Wormtongue's bewitchment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:28.960
But apart from his slip-up with the White&nbsp;
Wizard, he's actually a faithful soldier.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:34.800
In the books, Háma releases Éomer from prison,&nbsp;
arrests Wormtongue, and brings the King of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:40.240
the Golden Hall his sword. From there, Háma&nbsp;
follows his lord to the Battle of Helm's Deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:45.280
where he tragically dies in the fighting. His&nbsp;
last words come in conversation with a fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:51.200
soldier regarding Théoden's deposed advisor and&nbsp;
the temporarily absent Gandalf. When the fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:56.400
says that Gandalf's eccentric behavior could&nbsp;
be explained by Wormtongue, Háma's response is,

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.880
"True enough, but for myself, I will&nbsp;
wait until I see Gandalf again."

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:05.360
In the movie version, Háma doesn't&nbsp;
make it as far as the battle. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.800
Captain of the King's Guard is&nbsp;
killed on the way to Helm's Deep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.640
His last words come when he's busy&nbsp;
scouting ahead of the Rohirrim column.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.120
"What is it? Háma?"

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:17.600
"I'm not sure."

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:22.160
A few seconds later, he's&nbsp;
knocked over by a Warg-riding orc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:24.720
whose mount eats the loyal warrior face first.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:30.640
Madril is a character that was purely invented&nbsp;
for the Peter Jackson film adaptations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:35.600
In The Two Towers, the Gondorian officer&nbsp;
witnesses the events in Ithilien, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:41.760
Faramir's forces ambush the Haradrim and then&nbsp;
take Frodo, Sam, and eventually Gollum prisoner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:46.800
After that, Madril heads back with the troop&nbsp;
of rangers when they return to Osgiliath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:52.160
In The Return of the King, he can be seen helping&nbsp;
Faramir prepare the hopeless defense of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:58.000
ruined city. When the invading forces, led by&nbsp;
the disfigured Gothmog, swarm across the river,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.320
they overrun the garrison with little effort.

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:06.160
Amongst the Gondorian casualties is Madril,&nbsp;
whose last onscreen words are as a faithful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:12.240
second in command. After saving his captain&nbsp;
from his Orc pursuers, he advises his lord,

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.400
"We can't hold them. The city is lost."

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:20.080
Not long afterward, Madril is knocked down&nbsp;
by his enemies and receives a cold-blooded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:24.320
spear to the heart from Gothmog as he&nbsp;
lays helplessly in his enemy's path.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:30.720
While not technically dead, Frodo Baggins&nbsp;
absolutely deserves to be on a list of last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:34.480
words from the heroes of Middle-earth.&nbsp;
At the end of The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:39.840
the Ring-bearer sails away into blissful&nbsp;
retirement in the Undying Lands. But the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:45.520
is that he does so because he's so spiritually&nbsp;
devastated by the ordeal of carrying the Ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:48.720
that he basically can't endure&nbsp;
life in Middle-earth anymore.

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:54.640
As Frodo prepares to leave, he tells Sam that&nbsp;
although he was successful in saving the Shire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:58.880
the peaceful life that he once lived&nbsp;
is no longer available to him. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:02.560
as he gives Sam the book he's written&nbsp;
about their adventures, he adds,

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:05.280
"The last pages are for you, Sam."

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:11.840
In the book, after explaining why he's realized&nbsp;
he cannot remain in the Shire, Frodo tells his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:17.920
ever-loyal gardener and best friend that he is&nbsp;
his heir and must stay behind with his family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:23.040
Frodo then finishes with an invitation for Sam&nbsp;
to come and see him off at the harbor, saying,

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:25.840
"Come now, ride with me!"

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:31.200
The Lord of the Rings may not be Bilbo&nbsp;
Baggins' story, but the entire adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:36.240
wouldn't have taken place if it weren't for&nbsp;
his epic adventure half a century beforehand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:40.720
By the end of The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;
Bilbo is a very old Hobbit who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:44.400
waning quickly without the power of&nbsp;
the One Ring to keep him from aging.

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:49.920
The Hobbit hero leaves his comfy retirement pad&nbsp;
in Rivendell at the end of The Return of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:55.520
King. He's feeling very old at this point, but&nbsp;
he rallies his strength to head overseas to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.520 --> 00:23:00.400
immortal Blessed Realm — an honor he's earned&nbsp;
due to his legacy as one of the Ring-bearers.

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:06.880
In the Return of the King book, Bilbo's last line&nbsp;
comes when he meets Frodo and Sam on their way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:13.360
the Grey Havens. Upon seeing them, Bilbo proudly&nbsp;
declares to Frodo that, thanks to the help of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:19.120
the One Ring, he has finally become the oldest&nbsp;
Hobbit of all time, surpassing even the lifespan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:24.400
of the Old Took. His last line immediately&nbsp;
follows this statement as he tells his cousin,

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:28.880
"And now, I think I am quite ready to&nbsp;
go on another journey. Are you coming?"

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:34.320
In the movie adaptation, the line doesn't&nbsp;
come until they actually reach the harbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:37.360
at which point the elderly Bilbo proclaims,

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:42.480
"I think I'm quite ready for another adventure."

